      "name": "An IG site, built from the AST on its cache branch",
      "summary": "How an IG repository's own site is published, and how the AST it is built from is kept current (skill `ig-build-pipeline` §\"The AST cache lifecycle\").",
      "description": "How an IG repository's own site is published, and how the AST it is built from is kept current (skill `ig-build-pipeline` §\"The AST cache lifecycle\"). Owner, 2026-10-14: \"i want AST to be used. it should be in cache branch\", and \"that should be in fhir-harness then smart-* all use that\".\n\nTHE RULE. An IG site is built from the IG Publisher AST read from the IG repository's cache branch `cat/fhir-harness/fhir-ast/<package id>`, never from Publisher HTML. The publish refuses an AST that is not valid for the commit it publishes (`ig-cache.sh restore` then `verify`). A later commit that changes no IG input (`sushi-config.yaml`, `ig.ini`, `input/`) does not make the AST stale.\n\nTHE REFRESH. The cache is written by ONE thing: fhir-harness's reusable workflow `.github/workflows/ig-ast-refresh.yml`, which an IG repository's Site publish calls as its first job (`ast`; template `templates/ig-repo-site/site-publish.yml`). It runs the Publisher through the AST exporter on a GitHub runner (Java, a 10 GB heap, tx.fhir.org), because an agent's container cannot. It seeds the branch only from a commit on the default branch whose inputs equal the tip's.\n\nWHAT IT DOES NOT CLAIM. A valid AST is a cache built from these inputs; it is not a release. Every page drawn from it carries the provisional mark (`ig-ast-delta`).",
